Datum Flow's solver, mesher driver and results code are its own. It builds on these
open-source parts, each used under its own licence:

Engine (Rust crates)
- spade (MIT OR Apache-2.0, https://github.com/Stoeoef/spade): constrained Delaunay
  triangulation and refinement, under the 2D mesher.
- faer (MIT, https://github.com/sarah-quinones/faer-rs): sparse LU factorisation for the
  linear systems.
- serde and serde_json (MIT OR Apache-2.0): reading and writing the .dflow file.

Desktop shell (Rust crates)
- Tauri 2 and tauri-plugin-dialog (MIT OR Apache-2.0, https://tauri.app).
- ureq (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and sha2 (MIT OR Apache-2.0): the update check and the checksum
  of a downloaded installer.
- chrono (MIT OR Apache-2.0): dates on recovery files and the crash log.

Window
- SolidJS (MIT, https://www.solidjs.com).
- Chart.js (MIT, https://www.chartjs.org): the residual, monitor and line plots.
- IBM Plex Sans and IBM Plex Mono (SIL Open Font Licence 1.1), packaged by Fontsource.

Colour maps
- viridis, inferno and cividis: perceptually uniform colour maps released to the public
  domain (CC0) by their authors (S. van der Walt, N. Smith; J. Nuñez, C. Anderton, R. Renslow).

The full licence text of every crate is in its source package (see Cargo.lock for the exact
versions); `cargo deny check` in CI allows only the licences listed in deny.toml.

